#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f1b0944-5746-51e3-95af-0c78d353b0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251078} {:answer/id #uuid "5483cc68-a72b-52f6-99ad-108af7c29b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251245} {:answer/id #uuid "712f686d-666e-5b50-837d-069f1c8844f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251478} {:answer/id #uuid "831c3d8d-8d9b-5446-847d-4a9578ff7cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251659} {:answer/id #uuid "da79cc7e-e889-5625-9d36-6e8c5b69befe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "831c3d8d-8d9b-5446-847d-4a9578ff7cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251659} :answer-field/id #uuid "6264318d-7488-52ce-83d2-d05d6144f903" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64a6785a-7bbf-5956-bb67-37d306305466" :question/math-academy-id "q-118776" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=3+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2(3+0)}-\\frac{1}{2(3+{a}^{2})}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ed6a20c-d3bb-592f-8e4f-a620b4dc19e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285380} {:answer/id #uuid "75e76cf0-383c-5fca-99ea-28387d4e5368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285887} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc4a679-5371-540b-a4ee-e82ab4ebc0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286068} {:answer/id #uuid "95355a0d-4348-58b2-ac15-b501e96ac9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286144} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a5c260-a436-5bf1-aa3a-179f0a2476b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ed6a20c-d3bb-592f-8e4f-a620b4dc19e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285380} :answer-field/id #uuid "e01f0e05-7912-5f4f-a1e6-e67a3934581a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b5dd8ac-fb74-5814-828f-f32767c78b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-20797" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{3}$,  then $\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}+C \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{({e}^{{x}^{3}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d3be81-fa17-5678-892f-649528299dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319449} {:answer/id #uuid "ba22ce2a-0962-5777-b986-98a5bafb7d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba22ce2a-0962-5777-b986-98a5bafb7d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535718} :answer-field/id #uuid "efd6a557-0dd5-5de4-9841-579dcad1aa36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cdd21e7-31ed-571d-8af2-2513392df808" :question/math-academy-id "q-351605" :question/problem "Find the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ and the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](images/2b/2b7b85dd8894c73275816a8ee08a8c8a41fb41a327d20ed7bedc489f7e2e55ff.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{2}$, then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{3}))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fbbcb6c-2e6a-5a12-9d2e-23b23de5892e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330675} {:answer/id #uuid "6400598e-ce3c-555e-ba3f-3969e7f485d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330838} {:answer/id #uuid "703491e6-04b7-509f-b24e-1423bfa7f4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330956} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d34bc7-ffea-5420-9a4b-671380a6ae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331673} {:answer/id #uuid "cecf1f59-e403-5aa0-a3f9-39fb9dc1ad4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "703491e6-04b7-509f-b24e-1423bfa7f4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330956} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe4f3fe-a5f0-5d9d-abc1-a06051457234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55c54cd7-0604-5984-8b9b-055f20084d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-5966" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd286aa5384dae22d8fff4bb29799078547064926697d71b0b0ca8739641408f.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{6}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{6}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e74ed4-4bf4-58a5-82f1-bb5ffa3a7eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535474} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbbf7a3-60c8-55b3-8fe4-dad2f3bdc622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535545} {:answer/id #uuid "6899860b-81ea-5720-98fc-80926761f0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535599} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb453c5-9589-5071-99e3-645a2efd0834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535747} {:answer/id #uuid "e03abd1a-722a-5b07-a324-580b73ea930e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bbbf7a3-60c8-55b3-8fe4-dad2f3bdc622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535545} :answer-field/id #uuid "da97b470-1a5e-578a-80de-9d4315049c8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21f7ba2a-9a0f-57d4-9aff-030342b38301" :question/math-academy-id "q-86453" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the second of these integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=1+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1+0)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
